#
# prima-materia — the Chinese divinatory and siting transmission
#
# Order of the file: the tradition and the route that had to be decided before
# its first node; then the stalk counting of the Changes with what it is said
# to do and the one condition the canon attaches to it; then the counting of
# the Supreme Mystery, which states four conditions the Changes do not; then
# the siting of graves with its claim, its interval and the six adversities
# that absorb its failures; and last the two disputes, one against the oracle
# and one against the siting, both carried by works read at the wording.
#
# Added on 2026-09-10, after the burial book was read through: the five
# mountains on which one may not bury, with the premise stated for each; the
# form doctrine with its named outcomes, which is the longest run of decidable
# sentences this graph holds about any practice; the annotation printed
# beneath the text, which meets several of those outcomes with named cases the
# other way; and the account the imperial editors give of how the book that is
# being cited throughout this file was assembled.
#
# Added in the same batch, from five further chapters of the first-century
# treatise: the dispute over whether the chosen day decides anything, and the
# dispute over whether any outcome could count against the art at all. The
# second carries a description of an immunising regress, made from the outside
# and six hundred years before the recension cited here, of the machinery the
# burial book carries from the inside at its sixth adversity. A third chapter
# of the same treatise enters as an older second side at the dispute over the
# premise.
#
# The tradition instance was registered in traditions/registry-east-asian.ttl
# and is moved here now that passages have been read.
#
# Opened from the testability entrance of SPEC §14. The registration itself
# said why: claims here attach to datable outcomes. That turned out to be
# right and to have a second half the registration did not have — the same
# corpus carries the machinery that keeps the outcomes from ever counting.
#

pmt:ChineseDivination a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chinese divinatory and siting transmission"@en, "Chinesische Divinations- und Siedlungslehre"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Chinese transmission of divination by counting stalks down to a remainder, of a parallel counting on a different modulus, and of the siting of graves on the doctrine that the bones of the parents receive the qi of the ground and that the descendants are shaded by it."@en ;
    skos:note "Chosen from the registry on the testability entrance, and the reading bore the registration out on both halves. Fristen and stated outcomes are dense here: the burial book says that where the configuration is inauspicious the misfortune does not turn a day, and it names what a given shape of ground brings to the descendants, down to the extinction of the line. What the registration did not say is that the same book carries six adversities under which a favourable site produces nothing, and that the commentary on the sixth enumerates the delays, disputes, poverty and wars through which a burial fails to happen at all. Every non-occurrence has a place to go."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and the dispute is in the literature rather than in the sources. The graph already holds three transmissions that read a figure off an ungoverned remainder — the Ifá casting, the Fa casting and the geomantic tradition — and the counting of the Changes has the same form on a different modulus. One side derives the Arabic sand art from Chinese material through Needham's transmission routes; the other reconstructs it inside Abbasid Mesopotamia and states that the Chinese derivation was tried and dropped. Neither side was read at the wording in this run, and nothing here decides it; what the value records is that the question is contested and open, which is the opposite of what absent asserted."@en ;
    skos:note "A second reason the old value could not stand. The works read span the first century before the common era to a redaction of the fourteenth: the burial book as read is the recension Wu Cheng made from the eight sections Cai Yuanding had left of twenty, and it is first attested in the Song bibliography. Absent asks whether a route was available at the time the corpus was fixed, and for that end of the corpus the answer is not the same as for the Xici."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording, in the Chinese: Zhouyi, Xici A, sections on the great expansion and on what the Changes are without; Zhouyi, hexagram 4; Taixuanjing, Xuan shu, the eleventh; Zangshu, the whole of the single juan of the Siku recension, inner, outer and miscellaneous sections with its interlinear commentary; Lunheng 24, the treatise on divining; Jiu Tangshu 79, the memorial on the burial books. The Taixuanjing outside the counting chapter, and the rest of the Lunheng, were searched by term and not read continuously."@en ;
    skos:note "Read at the wording on 2026-09-10, in addition to the passages above and all of them in the Lunheng: the treatise on frugal burial and the treatise on the four taboos in juan 23, the treatises on censuring days, on distinguishing calamities and on objecting to the year star in juan 24, the treatise on interrogating the art in juan 25, and the fourth and last chapter of juan 23, which argues against the year and month taboos on breaking ground. With that the chapters of the work bearing on the arts this file holds are read at the wording; the remainder of the Lunheng, and the Taixuanjing outside the counting chapter, are still searched by term only."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition note. The Changes were read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; that text names no printing, and the citations say so rather than inventing one. The Taixuanjing and the Lunheng were read in the Sibu congkan facsimiles, the Zangshu and the Jiu Tangshu in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhouyi with the Xici commentary; Yang Xiong, Taixuanjing; Zangshu, in the recension of Wu Cheng; Wang Chong, Lunheng; the memorials of Lü Cai in Jiu Tangshu 79" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, Xici A, the section beginning with the number of the great expansion, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" .

#
# The counting of the Changes
#

pmp:Dieshi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The counting of the yarrow stalks"@en, "Das Abzählen der Schafgarbenstengel"@de ;
    skos:definition "The number of the great expansion is fifty, and forty-nine of them are used. They are divided into two to image the two; one is hung between the fingers to image the three; they are counted through by fours to image the four seasons; the remainder is returned between the fingers to image the intercalary month, and because five years hold two intercalations there are two such returns before the next hanging. Four operations complete a change, eighteen changes complete a hexagram, and the eight trigrams are a small completion."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter as it stands does not say what the remainder means. It gives four operations and eighteen changes and nowhere says that three changes make one line, nor how a remainder is read off as a value; the string for three changes does not occur in the section at all. The rule that makes the procedure executable is later, and stands in the Song commentary rather than in the canon. This file records what the canon gives and leaves the completion to the issue that carries it, because a node that printed the Song rule as canonical would report as ancient a step the classic does not take."@en ;
    skos:note "Two numbers stand in the same passage and only one of them is the famous one. The stalks of the two parts are eleven thousand five hundred and twenty, matching the number of the things; that is in the text. Four thousand and ninety-six is not, and belongs to a commentary."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, Xici A, the section beginning with the number of the great expansion, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" .

pmc:JishuZhilai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Exhausting the numbers to know what is coming"@en, "Die Zahlen erschöpfen, um das Kommende zu wissen"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the Changes are said to do, stated twice in the same treatise. To exhaust the numbers and thereby to know what is coming is called prognostication, and what yin and yang do not sound out is called spirit. And: the Changes are without thought and without doing, still and unmoving; touched, they thereupon penetrate the grounds of everything under heaven. The same section says that whoever is not the most spiritlike thing under heaven could not do this, and that the Changes receive a charge as an echo receives a sound."@en ;
    skos:note "The formulation is the strongest in this file and the least examinable, and both for the same reason. Without thought and without doing means that nothing about the operator's state is offered as a condition, so no failure can be assigned to him; and knowing what is coming is not tied to any statement that could turn out false. What the corpus does supply against this is the one condition on repetition, below, and the counter-argument from a work of the first century, held as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, Xici A, the sections on prognostication and on what the Changes are without, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" .

pmc:DieshiYieldsZhilai a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The counting yields the knowledge of what is coming"@en, "Das Abzählen bringt das Wissen des Kommenden hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Dieshi ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JishuZhilai ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, Xici A, the section beginning with the number of the great expansion and the section on prognostication, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" .

pmc:ZaisanDu a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Asking twice and three times defiles, and defiled it does not tell"@en, "Zwei- und dreimal fragen entweiht, und entweiht antwortet es nicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The judgement on the fourth hexagram states the only condition the canon attaches to consulting: at the first consultation it tells; asked twice and three times, that is defilement, and defiled it does not tell. The commentary on the judgement repeats the words and glosses the defilement as defiling the ignorance itself."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the node that decides how far this transmission can be examined at all, and it is worth having precisely because it is not a procedural rule. Nothing here says that a second casting is void, or wrong, or that the operator has erred. It says that repeating the question is an offence and that the answer is then withheld. The obvious test of any oracle is to put the same question twice and compare; the canon forbids exactly that and attaches no cost to the doctrine when the second answer differs, because by its own rule there should not be a second answer."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a caution and not as a prerequisite because nothing is said to be reached or missed. The consequence is stated, unusually for this corpus, and the file records it in the definition for want of a predicate; see prima-materia#621."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Dieshi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, hexagram 4, the judgement and the commentary on the judgement, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" .

#
# The counting of the Supreme Mystery, which states what the Changes do not
#

pmp:XuanShu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The counting of the Supreme Mystery"@en, "Das Abzählen des Höchsten Geheimnisses"@de ;
    skos:definition "A parallel counting on a different modulus. Thirty-six are looked to for the stalks; heaven divides by three and ends at six completions, hence eighteen, and since heaven does not give and earth does not complete, the number is doubled and earth leaves three empty against heaven's eighteen. One is separated and hung on the little finger of the left hand, the rest is divided in the middle and searched through by threes, and the remainder is set beside the notch. After one notch the remainder is counted: seven counts as one, eight as two, nine as three, and at six reckonings the way of the stalks is exhausted. The four positions so obtained give one of eighty-one heads, and each head carries nine appraisals ranked as thought, fortune and misfortune, each in a lower, middle and upper degree."@en ;
    skos:note "The interest is the contrast with the Changes, and it is exact: the same act of reading a figure off an ungoverned remainder, on a modulus of three instead of four, over four positions instead of six, into eighty-one figures instead of sixty-four. Two traditions in one language, three centuries apart, differing in the arithmetic and agreeing in the form. That is the strongest internal evidence this file holds that the form is not the content."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Yang Xiong, Taixuanjing, Xuan shu, the eleventh, with the commentary of Fan Wang, juan 8, Sibu congkan facsimile" .

pmc:FanshiYoudao a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The four conditions under which one does not consult"@en, "Die vier Bedingungen, unter denen nicht befragt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The same chapter states four conditions, and they are the ones the Changes leave unsaid. There is a way in consulting: who is not gathered does not consult; who does not doubt does not consult; who is not within the rule does not consult; and who will not act on the prognostication had better not have consulted. Before the casting a charge is spoken twice over, borrowing the Supreme Mystery and putting the matter in doubt before the spirits."@en ;
    skos:note "The fourth condition is the one that does work a graph can use. It makes the consultation pointless where the enquirer will not follow the answer, which is a statement about the enquirer and not about the world; and it thereby rules out the cheapest test of a divination, which is to consult and then do the opposite. Together with the prohibition on asking twice in the Changes, this transmission has now been found to close both of the two obvious tests, in two different works, by two different means."@en ;
    skos:note "The second condition cuts the other way and is worth keeping separate: who does not doubt does not consult. That excludes the control case in which a consultation is made about a question whose answer is already known, and a control case is what an examination of this practice would need first."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:XuanShu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Yang Xiong, Taixuanjing, Xuan shu, the eleventh, with the commentary of Fan Wang, juan 8, Sibu congkan facsimile" .

#
# The siting of graves
#

pmp:Zang a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Burial that rides the living qi"@en, "Bestattung, die das lebendige Qi reitet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The book opens with the sentence that burying is riding the living qi. The five qi run within the earth and issue forth to generate the ten thousand things; the qi scatters when it rides the wind and halts where it is bounded by water, and because the ancients gathered it so that it would not scatter and moved it so that it would halt, the thing is called wind and water. The living is a gathering of qi which congeals into bone and remains alone after death, so that burying is the method of returning the qi into the bone in order to shade what it generated."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, inner section, opening, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" .

pmc:YitiShouyin a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The bones receive the qi and the descendants are shaded"@en, "Die Gebeine empfangen das Qi und die Nachkommen werden beschirmt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim the whole doctrine rests on: a person receives his body from his parents, and when the root bones obtain the qi the body left behind is shaded. The classic is quoted for it — the qi is touched and responds, and the fortune of the dead reaches the living — and the resonance is illustrated by the bronze mountain that collapsed in the west while the numinous bell answered in the east."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is transitive across a generation and across death, which is what makes it examinable in principle: it predicts something about descendants from something about a grave. What it does not carry is a way of telling the shading from its absence in any single case, and the commentary printed with the text takes the transitivity further rather than bounding it, extending the shading to adopted children and to the sons of a second mother."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, inner section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" .

pmc:ZangYieldsYin a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Burial on the living qi yields the shading of the descendants"@en, "Die Bestattung auf dem lebendigen Qi bringt die Beschirmung der Nachkommen hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Zang ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:YitiShouyin ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, inner section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" .

pmc:HuoBuXuanri a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The misfortune does not turn a day"@en, "Das Unheil dreht keinen Tag"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one interval the book states. Where the configuration is favourable and the shape unfavourable, one may still hope for a single day of fortune; where the configuration is unfavourable and the shape favourable, the misfortune does not turn a day. The commentary says that this is put so strongly in order to say how fast the answer comes, and elsewhere the book has a burial made at the hollow in the morning and wept for in the evening."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the most examinable sentence found in the transmission and the reason the corpus was opened from the testability side. A doctrine that placed its effects a generation away could never be caught; this one places one class of them within a day of the burial, and the burial is a public and dated event."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, outer section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" .

pmc:SanjiLiuxiong a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Three felicities and six adversities"@en, "Drei Glücksfälle und sechs Unglücksfälle"@de ;
    skos:definition "The book closes with three conditions under which a hollow is felicitous and six under which the burial is adverse. The six: a discordance of yin and yang; a going wrong of the year and the season; a small strength that plans a great thing; a reliance on present fortune and standing power; an overreaching above or a falling short below; and a strange appearance among the responses. The classic is quoted for the conclusion that a felicitous hollow with an adverse burial is the same as abandoning the corpse."@en ;
    skos:note "The sixth adversity is the reason this node stands here and not among the ordinary cautions. The commentary printed with it enumerates what the strange appearances are, and the list is a list of ways in which nothing happens: the site is right and the family delays the burial; a quarrel prevents it; poverty and sickness together make it impossible; the master dies before he can return; the coffin stands for years and a war intervenes; the descendants do not agree; a lawsuit or a prison ends it; the days pass in neglect until the thing is abandoned. Every one of these is a reason a favourable grave produced no fortune, and every one is available after the fact."@en ;
    skos:note "The second adversity is separately consequential, because it is the one an outside witness attacked with a count. It requires the year and the season of the burial to be right, and the memorial recorded in the dynastic history sets against that a tally of burials in the classical annals on the day the burial books call most inauspicious."@en ;
    skos:note "What the graph gains here is not a warning but a shape of claim. Beside the interval that does not turn a day, this file now holds the machinery that keeps that interval from ever being read as a refutation, in the same book and a few folios apart."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Zang ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" .

#
# The two disputes
#

pmc:ZhanbuDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the stalks and the shell answer at all"@en, "Ob Stengel und Schale überhaupt antworten"@de ;
    skos:definition "A treatise of the first century sets itself against the claim that the Changes penetrate the grounds of everything under heaven. Its arguments, in the order it gives them: the names of the stalk and the shell mean the aged and the old, so that consulting them means consulting the aged, and neither is spiritlike; heaven, if it has no ears, is not a body but a vapour, and a vapour cannot tell anybody anything; the dead stalk and the dead shell are asked about the living; and, reading the very passage of the great expansion, the division into two images heaven and earth, the counting by fours images the seasons and the remainder images the intercalation, which is imaging by likeness in order to set up the number of the figures, and says nothing about heaven and earth answering a man. Its own account of the hits is coincidence: what the shell is said to meet and the stalks to encounter is what was met with, not what was brought about, and following the answer does not necessarily bring fortune nor going against it misfortune."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a dispute and not as a refutation because that is what it is: two works, one asserting and one denying, both read at the wording in this run, neither carrying a case. The value of having it as a node is that the denying side reads the same passage the asserting side rests on, and reads it as a construction by analogy rather than as a channel."@en ;
    skos:note "The strongest move in the denying work is not an argument but a condition, and it is held separately as a protocol in examinations/. It is not in this node because a dispute records two positions and a condition is a third kind of thing."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:JishuZhilai ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, Xici A, the sections on prognostication and on what the Changes are without, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing", "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on divining, juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile" .

pmc:ZangshuDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the choice of grave and day decides the fortune of the descendants"@en, "Ob die Wahl von Grab und Tag das Geschick der Nachkommen entscheidet"@de ;
    skos:definition "A memorial preserved in the dynastic history sets seven numbered objections against the burial books, on an imperial commission to revise the divinatory literature. The rites fix the interval between death and burial by rank, so the burial has a set time and no year or month is to be chosen; the books call one day of the sexagenary cycle the most inauspicious of all for a burial, and a check of the classical annals turns up more than twenty burials on that day, so the day is not chosen either; the hour is fixed by what each dynasty honoured, so the hour is not chosen; one house kept its succession and another lost its sacrifices without either turning on a burial; the ancients buried north of their capitals in one fixed place, without the surname doctrine, and their descendants were not the poorer for it; a grave once made is never altered while rank rises and falls without rest, so office is not what a burial confers; and the practice makes mourners choose ground in hope of an office and a season in hope of money, so that the books corrupt the customs."@en ;
    skos:note "The sixth objection is the one worth naming, because it is an argument of a form the graph can reuse: the cause is invariant and the effect fluctuates, therefore the cause is not that cause. The second is of another form again and is treated as evidence rather than as testimony, in the protocol beside this file."@en ;
    skos:note "The defending side is the book itself and the commentary printed with it, which cites two cases the other way: a reader of graves who saw a general's tomb and said the clan would be wiped out, and it was; and a duke of the annals who moved his capital against a prognostication that it would benefit the people and not the ruler, and died in the fifth month. Both are anecdotes reported by the defence and neither was traced to the works they name."@en ;
    skos:note "A second and much older objector was added on 2026-09-10, and it attacks the premise rather than the practice. The first-century treatise on frugal burial argues that the dead have no knowledge: that while a parent lives his child seeks the diviner and calls the physician, in hope that the misfortune may be dispelled and the drug do good, but that once he is dead, though the diviner were as discerning as Wu Xian and the physician as skilled as Bian Que, he does not live again, because with the qi cut off there is no further benefit at all; and that the soul of a dead parent assuredly knows nothing. On that account there is no channel from the bones to the grandson, which is the premise this file holds as the ground of the whole art. The treatise is arguing against lavish burial and not against siting, so the objection is a consequence of its position and not its target; that is why it is entered here as a side and not as its own dispute."@en ;
    skos:note "The two objectors do not agree with each other and the graph should not merge them. The memorial concedes that a grave is chosen and argues that the choice decides nothing; the treatise denies the mechanism that would make any choice matter. A defender who has answered one has not answered the other."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:YitiShouyin ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, inner and miscellaneous sections, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text", "Jiu Tangshu 79, the memorial of Lü Cai on the burial books, juan 79, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text", "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on frugal burial (Bo zang), juan 23, Sibu congkan facsimile" .

#
# The form doctrine, and the commentary that undoes it
#

pmc:WuBukezang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The five mountains on which one may not bury"@en, "Die fünf Berge, auf denen nicht bestattet werden darf"@de ;
    skos:definition "Five prohibitions, each stated as a consequence of a premise the book has already laid down. Because the qi comes about through the harmonious generation of things, a bald mountain may not be buried on. Because the qi comes by way of the form, a broken mountain may not be buried on. Because the qi travels by way of the earth, a stone mountain may not be buried on. Because the qi is stopped by the configuration, a mountain that merely passes through may not be buried on. Because the qi gathers where the dragons meet, a solitary mountain may not be buried on. The classic is then quoted for the conclusion: bald, broken, stone, passing and solitary generate new misfortune and extinguish the fortune already there."@en ;
    skos:note "The five are the only prohibitions in this book whose ground is stated, and each ground is a clause of the doctrine rather than a rule of its own. That makes them the place where the transmission is most nearly deductive: given what it has said the qi is, these five follow, and a reader who accepted the premises and rejected a prohibition would be contradicting himself and not merely disagreeing."@en ;
    skos:note "The closing formula is stronger than a warning and is why this is entered as one. It does not say that such a burial fails to help; it says that it generates new misfortune and cancels fortune the family already had, so the claim is that the operation done wrongly is worse than not done."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Zang ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, inner section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" .

pmc:XingshiJixiong a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The configuration and the form, each with a named outcome"@en, "Gestalt und Lage, jede mit benanntem Ausgang"@de ;
    skos:definition "The book's longest run of decidable sentences, in two series. By configuration: like ten thousand horses coming down from heaven, the burial of a king; like great waves with ridge behind ridge, the burial of a lord of a thousand chariots; like a descending dragon with the water winding and the clouds attending, rank and emolument of the three highest offices; like storeyed houses with lush grass and tall trees, the opening of a government and the founding of a state; like a startled snake, crooked and slanting, the ruin of house and state; like spear and halberd, death by arms and imprisonment of the form; like running water, the living all become ghosts. By form: like a screened seat with a ridge standing in the middle, buried where it stops, princes and lords arise; like a swallow's nest, buried at the bend, land is granted and rank divided; like a fallen mound with the ridge coming from far behind and the answer in front curving round, the nine thorns and three sophoras of high office; like an upturned cauldron, its ridge can make rich; like a planted cap, lasting prosperity and joy; like scattered counting rods, all affairs are confounded; like disordered clothing, jealous daughters and licentious wives; like a bag of ashes, the house burns and the granary with it; like an upturned boat, the women fall ill and the men go to prison; like a crosswise stool, the sons are cut off and the grandsons die; like a lying sword, execution and extermination; like an upturned knife, misfortune and flight into hiding. The series closes with rules over the two: where configuration and form run together it is auspicious and where they run against each other inauspicious; a good configuration with a bad form leaves one hope in a hundred; a bad configuration with a good form brings the misfortune before the day turns. And of the whole art it says that the configuration is the hardest to judge, the form next, and the compass direction last of all."@en ;
    skos:note "These are the most examinable single sentences this graph holds about any practice. Each names a visible feature of ground and a public, datable event in a family — an office, an execution, a fire, a line dying out — and the ground is there to be seen before the event. What decides whether they could ever be tested is not the outcome but the antecedent: who fixes which of these forms a given hill has, and when. The book itself says the configuration is the hardest of the three to judge, which is as close as it comes to conceding the point."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause about a bad configuration with a good form is a second interval claim beside the one this file already holds. It places the misfortune within a day, in a different pairing from the one at the node about the misfortune that does not turn a day, and the commentary glosses it as saying that the response is immediate. Two independent places of the same book therefore put one class of its effects inside twenty-four hours of the burial."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" .

pmc:ZangshuZhuDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "The commentary printed with the book contradicts the book's own outcomes"@en, "Der mitgedruckte Kommentar widerspricht den Ausgängen des Buches selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The annotation printed beneath the text in this edition does not defend the outcome sentences; at several of them it names cases going the other way and tells the reader not to follow the book. Where the text says that a form like a crosswise stool cuts off the sons and kills the grandsons, the note answers that where the true stool form is obtained it is a transformation of water and produces literary distinction and success in the examinations, and names three families whose ancestral graves were of that form: the forebear of a chief councillor Lu, of the prodigy Yang, and of a eunuch Fang. Where the text says that a form like a lying sword brings execution and extermination, the note answers that there are sword forms that have produced the eminent, and names one. Where the text places the flying serpent among the inauspicious, the note asks how many sites of serpent form have been used from antiquity to now, and whether they can all be set aside as dangerous. The same hand qualifies four of the five prohibited mountains in the same way, each time with the formula that there is a further kind which is not to be rejected on that ground."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a dispute inside one book and one printing, and the two sides are the text and its annotation. It is entered because the graph would otherwise carry the outcome sentences as the position of this transmission, when the transmission as it is actually handed on carries the sentences and their systematic qualification on the same page."@en ;
    skos:note "The form of the qualification is the part worth having. The note never denies the rule; it denies that the case at hand was an instance of it, on the ground that the form was only apparently that form. That move preserves every sentence of the text against every counter-example, and it is the same machinery this file already records at the six adversities, one level up: there the failures of a correct burial are absorbed, here the successes of an incorrect one."@en ;
    skos:note "Who wrote the annotation is not established, and the edition itself says so. The prefatory summary of the imperial collection states that Cai Yuanding cut the twenty pian to eight, that Wu Cheng afterwards divided what he had into the inner, outer and miscellaneous sections, that Liu Zezhang of Xinyu received the text from Wu and annotated it, and that whether the note in this printing is Liu's cannot be determined. The graph therefore has the counter-examples as an anonymous later hand and not as a named party."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:XingshiJixiong ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text", "Zangshu, the annotation printed beneath the miscellaneous and inner sections in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text of the Wu Cheng recension" .

pmc:ZangshuRecensionHistory a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "How the book read here was made"@en, "Wie das hier gelesene Buch entstanden ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The account the editors of the imperial collection give of the text they are printing. The attribution to Guo Pu of the fourth century is a received one and the book is not among the works his biography names; a burial book under his name first appears in the Song bibliography. Practitioners afterwards competed in embellishing it until it ran to twenty pian; Cai Yuanding, finding it overgrown, struck out twelve and kept eight; Wu Cheng, holding that Cai had not gone to the bottom of it, took what was purest as the inner section, what was half fine and half coarse as the outer, and what should have gone but was kept for the time being as the miscellaneous. Liu Zezhang of Xinyu received the text from Wu and annotated it, though whether the annotation printed here is his cannot be determined. The Song bibliography called it the burial book, and later admirers renamed it the burial classic, a change the editors decline to follow."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because every node in this file that cites the burial book cites a text of that history, and because the ordering is not a scholarly aside but the thing being cited. The material a reader meets as one doctrine was selected twice, by two named men four generations apart, on the criterion of purity; what was cut is not preserved, so the graph cannot say what the transmission said before the cuts."@en ;
    skos:note "The miscellaneous section carries the outcome sentences and the six adversities, that is to say the two bodies of material this file draws most heavily on. In Wu Cheng's own arrangement that is the section he judged coarse, kept provisionally and would have removed. The citations name the section for that reason."@en ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:XingshiJixiong, pmc:SanjiLiuxiong, pmc:WuBukezang, pmc:HuoBuXuanri ;
    pm:compiledBy "Cai Yuanding, who reduced twenty pian to eight; Wu Cheng, who divided the remainder into inner, outer and miscellaneous sections; and the editors of the Siku quanshu, who report both and settle the title" ;
    dcterms:date "1781" ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, the editors' prefatory summary in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text, dated the tenth month of the forty-sixth year of Qianlong" .

#
# The contemporary critic
#

pmc:ZeriDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the day chosen decides anything"@en, "Ob der gewählte Tag über irgendetwas entscheidet"@de ;
    skos:definition "A treatise of the first century attacks the choosing of days across four chapters, and the arguments are of three kinds. First, comparisons within the practice: encoffining, which puts the corpse in the box, is done on no chosen day, while burial, which puts the box in the ground, is; and grave, field and dwelling are equally the ground of a man's fortune, yet only the westward extension of a dwelling is forbidden. Second, a count: in the age of the annals the deaths of kings, lords and great officers ran to hundreds and thousands, and their burial days do not agree with the calendars; the rites fix the interval by rank, so that the parity of the month follows from the rank and not from a choice; and where a burial was postponed the annals give rain as the reason and not a day. Third, a case in which one day carries two outcomes: King Wu won his battle on a jiazi day and the tyrant lost his on the same jiazi day, the two armies facing one another with their standards in sight, one day and two fates. The same treatise adds that washing, combing, cutting cloth, building and moving house each have their own calendar, that water and wood are both of the five phases yet only the one is subject to a day, and that a man who kills is punished whatever day he chose."@en ;
    skos:note "The third argument is the one this graph should keep, because it is the only one that discriminates rather than compares. Two parties, one date, opposite outcomes: whatever the day is supposed to decide, it did not decide that. The others show that the practice is applied inconsistently, which embarrasses the practitioners without touching the doctrine."@en ;
    skos:note "It is a party and not an instance, and the difference matters for the claim it disputes. The treatise argues; it does not count anything it gathered itself, except the burials in the annals, and that count is a reading of a text and not a series of cases. Where it counts, it is evidence and belongs at a protocol; where it argues, it is a witness like the book it attacks, and that is how it is held here."@en ;
    skos:note "The dispute has a shape worth noting beside the burial book's own. The critic never says that the ground or the hour has no effect and then names what would show it; he shows that the rule is not followed where it would be if it were believed. That leaves the doctrine intact for anyone willing to say that his contemporaries were careless, which is the answer the burial book's own annotation gives in a different connection."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:SanjiLiuxiong ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text", "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on censuring days (Ji ri), juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile", "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on interrogating the art (Jie shu), juan 25, Sibu congkan facsimile", "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on the four taboos (Si hui), juan 23, Sibu congkan facsimile", "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on objecting to the year star (Nan sui), juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile" .

pmc:BiansuiDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether any outcome could count against the art"@en, "Ob irgendein Ausgang gegen die Kunst zählen könnte"@de ;
    skos:definition "The same treatise sets out, in the chapter on distinguishing calamities, what happens to a counter-example put to a practitioner of these arts, and it does so by carrying one out three times. Suppose ten people in one household who move nothing, break no ground, change no dwelling, and choose auspicious days for every sacrifice, marriage and journey from spring to winter, offending against no prohibition: in a hundred years, can they avoid dying? The diviner, the treatise says, will then answer that a dwelling has its flourishing and its decay, and that the year-breaker and the direct-symbol were not avoided. Suppose then that they consult the practitioners at each turn, stay where the dwelling flourishes and leave where it decays, and move the whole household whenever the year-breaker falls: in a hundred years, can they avoid dying? He will then answer that moving offends against the time and that going and coming were not auspicious. Suppose they consult on that too: in a hundred years, can they avoid dying? He will then answer that the allotted span was simply at an end. The treatise concludes that on that showing life and death have their own term after all, and that no offence against year or month brought them about. It names the interest behind the regress in the same chapter: seeing a misfortune, the practitioners of the art point to the taboo and say nothing of the rest; hearing of a fortune, they keep it back and let it go unreported; they heap up misfortunes to alarm the careless and set out fortunes to hearten those who fear the times. And it puts three mass cases against the doctrine: the city of Liyang, which sank in one evening and became a lake, whose people had not all offended against the year and the month; the forty thousand of the Zhao army buried alive at Changping, who had not all left home at an unchosen hour; and the thousand prisons of the empire with ten thousand inmates, who had not all transgressed a taboo."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the most valuable thing read in the treatise and the reason for opening it. It is a first-century description of an immunising regress, carried out step by step against a living practitioner, and it ends where such regresses end, at a clause that covers every remaining case. The graph holds the same machinery from the inside, at the six adversities of the burial book, whose annotation enumerates the ways in which nothing happens. Here it is described from the outside, six hundred years earlier, by someone who put it to a practitioner."@en ;
    skos:note "The second argument, about which cases the practitioners report, is a statement of selection in the record. It is the one argument in the treatise that a modern protocol could take over unchanged, and it is prior to any count: where only the confirmations are collected, a count of confirmations establishes nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "The three mass cases are weaker than they look and the treatise does not notice why. Each says that a large group shared an outcome without sharing a cause, which refutes a doctrine claiming that the cause is necessary; the burial book does not claim that, and its annotation would be free to say that each of the drowned of Liyang had his own reason. The regress above is the stronger instrument precisely because it needs no case at all."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:ZangYieldsYin ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text", "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on distinguishing calamities (Bian sui), juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile" .

#
# What the year and the month are said to eat, and the critic who weighed it
#

pmc:SuiyueSuoshi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The quarter the year and the month eat"@en, "Das Viertel, das Jahr und Monat verzehren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The doctrine of the common practice of the first century, as the treatise that attacks it reports it. When earth is broken and building begun, the year and the month each have a quarter they eat, and in the quarter eaten there will certainly be deaths. The example runs by the branches: with the year star at zi the year eats at you, and with the first month established at yin the month eats at si, so that work begun in the zi or the yin ground is eaten out of the households standing at you and at si. The harm is graded by the size of the work: a labour of three feet eats within one pace, a labour of ten zhang eats a li away. Those who find themselves eaten hang up against it an object of the phase that conquers, metal where the west is eaten and charcoal where the east is; or they set out a sacrifice to remove the ill; or they leave the dwelling empty and move away."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as its opponent reports it, and the citation says so. No transmission of the practice itself has been run down in this holding: what stands is a first-century critic's account of what his contemporaries did, quoted closely enough that the branch arithmetic can be checked against the doctrine's own rule. Where a doctrine enters a graph only through the man attacking it, the graph should say which sentences are his summary and which are his argument, and that is what the separation of this node from the dispute below is for."@en ;
    skos:note "The graded scale is the part worth keeping. It is the only quantitative claim in the material this file holds about the year and month taboos: it fixes a distance from a size, and it is what makes the counter-cases possible at all. Without the scale, the wall of Qin and the building of Luo would say nothing about the doctrine, because nothing would follow from the size of the work."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the fourth and last chapter of juan 23, against the year and month taboos on breaking ground, Sibu congkan facsimile" .

pmp:Yansheng a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Hanging up the conquering phase"@en, "Das Aufhängen der überwindenden Wandlungsphase"@de ;
    skos:definition "The countermeasure taken by a household that finds itself in the quarter eaten, as the same chapter reports it. An object of one of the five phases is hung up in the house: metal where the west is eaten, charcoal where the east is. Beside it stand two other remedies, a sacrifice set out to remove the ill and the vacating of the dwelling until the danger has passed. The chapter says the practice was copied from neighbour to neighbour and that everyone held it to be so."@en ;
    skos:note "The procedure is decided by direction alone. It names which phase is to be hung and says nothing of how much of it, which is the opening the critic goes through and the reason this practice is held as a node of its own rather than as a clause of the doctrine."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the fourth and last chapter of juan 23, against the year and month taboos on breaking ground, Sibu congkan facsimile" .

pmc:SuiyueSuoshiDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether a quarter can be eaten, and whether a hung object could stop it"@en, "Ob ein Viertel verzehrt werden kann, und ob ein aufgehängter Gegenstand es aufhielte"@de ;
    skos:definition "The attack the same chapter mounts, in arguments of four kinds and two cases. First, justice: the spirits of heaven and earth are of one mind, and to be punished without an offence is what the world calls a wrong; the man who breaks ground disturbs the body of the earth no less than the man who moves house, and yet it is the household at you, which has done nothing, that is eaten. Second, position and scale: the year star stands at the edge of heaven in the zi place while the builder stands within one province of the Middle Kingdom, and on Zou Yan's geography the whole of the known world lies in the southeast, so that a year eating at you eats the land of the western Qiang and the southeast can take no harm from it; and if the neighbour's house to the west is the you ground, then the builder's own compound has a you quarter too, which the spirit passes over in order to eat a stranger's. Third, the regress of spirits: if year and month have spirits, so has the day; days accumulate into months, months into seasons, seasons into years, of which one thousand five hundred and thirty-nine make a tong and four thousand six hundred and seventeen an era, and these are names for multiples and for the ends of reckonings and not for spirits; within a single day the twelve double-hours are laid on the same twelve branches and eat nothing, while the establishment of the month on those branches eats. Fourth, appetite and work: a spirit's mouth and belly are as a man's, and a man eats when he is hungry and stops when he is full, whereas this one eats only where building is done, so that in a lean age, with dwellings abandoned and building at an end, it would go hungry; and setting timber in a dug pit and raising a dike in a cut ditch are the same labour, yet the year eats the building of a house and not the working of a field. The two cases take the doctrine's own scale at its word: Meng Tian's wall for Qin used half the empire, so the disasters should be counted in tens of thousands, and the Qin people did not die in numbers; and the Duke of Zhou's building of Luo was work of the greatest extent, and what the record reports is that the people of the four quarters came together in concord, that the work was finished, and that no great mortality is heard of."@en ;
    skos:note "The argument against the countermeasure is the most transferable thing in the chapter, and it is not an objection from outside the doctrine. The conquest of the phases is granted: metal conquers wood, and yet wood beats metal where the wood is many and the metal few; pile up metal like a hill and light one charcoal under it and the metal will not melt. A cup of water on a burning Mount Tai, a handful of earth in a river breached for a thousand li, a five-foot child against Meng Ben. The doctrine fixes which phase conquers and says nothing of how much, while the practice chooses the object by direction alone; the mismatch is a defect in the doctrine's own terms."@en ;
    skos:note "The two cases have a different shape from the arguments and a weaker footing. Each is an argument from a silence in the record, and it is worth only what the record is worth. The same treatise says, in the chapter on distinguishing calamities that this file holds beside this one, that the practitioners of these arts keep the favourable cases back and report the unfavourable; a selection of that kind would work against an argument from what is not heard of just as well as it works against a count of confirmations."@en ;
    skos:note "The shape of this dispute is not the shape of the one over chosen days. There the critic shows that the rule is not followed where it would be if it were believed, which leaves the doctrine standing for anyone willing to call his contemporaries careless. Here he takes the rule at its word, reads a consequence off its own scale, and looks for the consequence in the record. That is the only place in this file where a Chinese fate art is met with a quantity it committed itself to."@en ;
    skos:note "Both sources cited are the critic's, and one of them is the chapter that carries the doctrine as well. That is not the usual case in this file, where a dispute cites the transmission on one side and its opponent on the other, and it is recorded rather than concealed: the practice's own transmission of the year and month taboos on breaking ground has not been located in this holding, so the minimum of two sources is met by two chapters of one author and not by two sides."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:SuiyueSuoshi, pmp:Yansheng ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    dcterms:source "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the fourth and last chapter of juan 23, against the year and month taboos on breaking ground, Sibu congkan facsimile", "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on objecting to the year star (Nan sui), juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile" .
